    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d564">

                <pb facs="0620" n="608" sameAs="frus:frus1941v02/pg608"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.91262/244: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Chargé in Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Morris</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-04-26T18:00:00-05:00">April 26, 1941—6
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1202. Reference Department’s instructions on the (1) Nelson and Jacobsen,<anchor
                        n="11" xml:id="d564-f1"/> (2) Allen, and (3) Hottelet cases. In presenting
                    these notes to the Foreign Office, you are requested to seek an interview with a
                    high official and to indicate orally that the continued detention in prison of
                    these American citizens without charges being formally presented against them is
                    in marked contrast to the treatment accorded certain German cases in the United
                    States where the accused persons are frequently released on bail. A continuation
                    of this practice on <pb facs="0621" n="609" xml:id="pg609"/> the part of German
                    authorities could ultimately react unfavorably for accused German citizens in
                    the United States. Furthermore, in the case of Allen, you may point out that the
                    time he has already spent in prison would appear to be sufficient punishment for
                    the offense, and this is particularly true in view of the German treatment of
                    similar cases. You may likewise state that the German crews accused of sabotage
                    are allowed frequent access to their consular representatives in the United
                        States.<anchor n="12" xml:id="d564-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>Please cable full report on German reaction to your representations.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d564-f1" n="11" xml:id="d564-f1-content">
                <p>Frank Nelson and Ivan Jacobsen, American citizens in Norway, were arrested by
                    German authorities in early December 1940, charged with being in possession of
                    documents hostile to the Government in Norway established under German
                    occupation.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d564-f2" n="12" xml:id="d564-f2-content">
                <p>For correspondence concerning arrest of crews of the <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Pauline Friedrich</hi> and the <hi rendition="#italic">Arauca</hi>, see
                        <ref target="frus1941v01#pg_451">vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>,
                        pp. 451</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
